WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.335
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.110
SPEAKER: Hello.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.920
Let's talk about one of the
most important roles of an API

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.990
program and agile--

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.120
the API product owner.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.910
In agile, the private
owner is a central role

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.100
within the team and the program.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:20.680
From defining the product
to guiding the API

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:22.960
team during a review
of finished work,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.500
everything should be in the
view of the API product owner.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.760
A great Venn diagram,
courtesy of Henrik Nyberg,

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.400
positions the product
owner role in the middle

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.890
of 3 responsibilities--

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.930
build the right thing, build the
thing right, and build it fast.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:43.090
The scrum definition
of product owner

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:46.630
translates very well
in the API context.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.750
The API product owner
is a key leadership role

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:53.080
when it comes to how API
products should be produced,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.830
and how it is being consumed
upstream in the digital value

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.180
chain.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.840
The product owner needs to
have a connected and empathic

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.290
relationship with developers,
experience designers,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.360
and businesses leads
that use the API

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.350
products to deliver on the
experiences for the customer.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.650
The API product owner
also has to keep a handle

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.070
on how the API team is
producing API products,

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.260
and how they're connecting
into the back end

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.090
systems of the enterprise.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:25.390
The API product owner
is often very busy,

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.330
and the role can be quite
challenging in the context

00:01:28.330 --> 00:01:30.040
switching that is required.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.590
For example, in the
morning, the product owner

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.570
may be speaking with
digital businesses leads

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.230
to plan new experiences
that require additional APIs

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.640
in the next release.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.500
Later that morning,
the product owner

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.680
may be working with the scrum
master and API architect

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.500
to manage the backlog and
prioritize which stories are

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.400
to be worked on in
the upcoming sprints.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.210
In the afternoon,
the product owner

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.450
may be reviewing the results
of a recent spring and the APIs

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.310
with developers and testers.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.440
In the last session
of the day, the

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.800
is meeting with APIs
architects and IT leadership

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.620
about potential changes that
need to be made downstream

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:09.100
to systems, because the targets
of the back end have changed.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.509
What a day.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:13.870
One of the key mindset
traits of product ownership

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:17.620
is to evangelize APIs
as truly products.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:20.620
The way the product owner
drives the communication,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.800
the packaging, the
documentation, and delivery

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:26.800
of each API, will
heavily influence how

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.650
APIs are consumed and produced.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:33.940
This might manifest in the form
of API roadshows, hackathons,

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.240
or building a really easy to
use and self-service developer

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:38.230
portal.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.470
To summarize, the
role more concretely,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.980
the API product owner,
or PO, as often called,

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.800
is responsible
for the following.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.440
Defining and delivering
value but making decisions

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.990
for the team on
what's more valuable,

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:56.200
defining the product
backlog, prioritizing

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.110
the sprint backlog,
defining requirements

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.810
as well as the definition
of ready and done,

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.940
and because of the
cross-functional demands,

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.400
a background in product
management and web

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.540
based technology
is a key skill set.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.270
The API product owner
has to be very involved

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.380
in the day to day
sprint process.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.040
However, depending on the
size of the organization

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.920
and the number of APIs in the
maturity of the API ecosystem,

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.038
this might not always be
feasible or practical.

00:03:24.038 --> 00:03:25.830
It's really important
for the product owner

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.860
to recognize when demands
from people and teams

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:32.620
become too great for the hours
a product owner has available.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.490
This self-awareness is critical
since the product owner

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.430
can often become a
bottleneck in the process.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.060
In addition, since the
API product owner also

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.400
needs to network across
the digital value

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.400
chain for upstream and
downstream stakeholders,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.860
the scope can simply
be too broad to go deep

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.020
with the development team.

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.540
In these cases, it is essential
that the product owner

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.320
figures out how and where
to delegate authority.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.680
One such example is
in sprint refinement.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.690
And refinement sessions
elaborate and use cases,

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.980
writing BDD use cases, and
collaborating real time

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:10.270
with the API architect,
developers, testers,

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:13.590
and scrum masters really
requires a pretty heavy focus

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:14.910
and time.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.790
If there are multiple API
teams or demands on building up

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:22.230
the API program buy in with
business and IT leadership,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.090
this job can be very intensive.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.000
The API product ownership
bring on a business sense

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.730
that can act as the proxy
to the product owner.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.490
It's important that the product
owner trains and transfers

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.830
ownership and decision making
authority to the business

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:35.640
analyst.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:37.830
The business analyst
must have the authority

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:40.980
to make design and
requirement decisions.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.400
If there are certain thresholds
that decisions cannot be made,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.220
those lines also need
to be made clear.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.400
Looking more strategically,
managing the backlog,

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.330
and the overall product roadmap
is also a critical activity

00:04:54.330 --> 00:04:56.210
for the product owner.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:58.850
However, when it comes
to backlog management,

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:01.550
the API product owner may not
want to give up full authority

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.840
to the business analyst.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.870
Certain priorities require
organizational perspective

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:09.560
to determine what should
be in an API release.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.680
These decisions may have
many strategic implications

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.600
for the overall
digital transformation,

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.390
and the overall API product
roadmap in orchestration

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:20.610
with upstream and
downstream projects.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.450
However, when it comes to
the sequence in which an API

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.300
or user story should be
built within a sprint

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.900
or sprint by sprint, a business
analyst in collaboration

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.360
with the scrum master
and API architect,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.400
can make the decisions
within the team.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.440
At the end of the
sprint, the features

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.110
develop should be up
for group demonstration.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.290
This is called a sprint
review, or sprint demo.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:46.070
This demo could be to the
team, to the end users like app

00:05:46.070 --> 00:05:48.500
developers, or with
other interested business

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.110
stakeholders and
executive sponsors.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:54.860
There are several motivations
to demo or do a review.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.620
They don't have to be all
crammed into one session,

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:00.260
and multiple demos
might make sense.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.320
Scrum provides
frame-working guidelines

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:06.800
but it's up to each team,
with the API product owner,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.940
to discuss and determine what is
the right audience and outcome.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:14.120
The product owner should make
the final decision on this one

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.670
as it relates heavily to
how the organization will

00:06:16.670 --> 00:06:18.860
perceive the API program.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.710
Here are some common
reasons for a sprint review.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.040
These demos can be run
by executing on a Postman

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:27.380
or Gherkin file, or
create a simple web

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:29.570
app called to show how
the API can be used

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:32.160
to illustrate a visual example.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.050
The one thing we caution against
is to use the demo as a sign

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:37.670
off stage gate.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.700
Demos are often too short
to allow any stakeholder, ,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.540
a product owner or tester,
to do a final acceptance.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:48.020
Furthermore, the time
spent to orchestrate a demo

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.140
and QA at the same time
can be very inefficient use

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.120
of the team's collective time.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.250
All testing acceptance
should happen

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.170
before the sprint review.

00:06:57.170 --> 00:07:00.320
If there isn't enough time,
don't rush the sprint review,

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.960
and do it after
the sprint is over.

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.850
Focus on the big picture.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.340
Use the demo to
garner excitement,

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:11.400
celebrate accomplishments,
and gather business feedback.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.690
Thank you.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:15.590
I hope this section helped
you get a deeper look

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:17.630
at the role of a product owner.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:20.930
It's a complex role, and
each product owner we meet

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:22.040
is different.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:25.340
And what you learn can be
adapted and modified based

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.140
on your style, context, .

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:28.950
And organization.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.970
In addition, the role can be
split between multiple people

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.960
owning different parts.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:37.230
There's not just one way if
you want more information,

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:41.000
please check out the community
for ebooks, articles,

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:43.460
and other related topics.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.700
Thank you again for watching.

